Package: AutoXplainR
Title: Guided Model Fitting, Evaluation, and Explanation
Version: 0.6.2
Authors@R:
    person(
      given = "Matteo",
      family = "Mazzarelli",
      email = "matteo.mazzarelli@gmail.com",
      role = c("aut", "cre")
    )
Copyright: 2025-2026 Matteo Mazzarelli
Description: Guides first-time modelers through fitting, held-out evaluation,
    explanation, and communication for tabular prediction problems. Local
    defaults compare linear, tree and neural model families with training-only
    cross-validation and a held-out evaluation. Offline tabbed reports connect
    model scores, measured costs, feature effects and input relationships.
    Training-only resampling can tune behaviorally
    diverse linear, regularized, additive, tree, forest, boosting, neural,
    kernel, nearest-neighbor, and multivariate adaptive regression spline families
    without opening the final holdout. Model-agnostic tools compare predictive
    disagreement and fitted reliance across families, and add repeated
    permutation importance, feature-dependence diagnostics, accumulated local
    effects, and diagnostics retaining settings and permutation draws. Optional adapters support
    'H2O' AutoML, interactive 'Plotly' graphics, and draft
    narratives for review. Whole-group and chronological holdouts preserve study
    boundaries; paired evaluation bootstraps quantify fixed-model sampling
    uncertainty. Accumulated local effects follow Apley and Zhu (2020)
    <doi:10.1111/rssb.12377>. Fitted model reliance and near-optimal-model
    comparisons draw on Fisher, Rudin, and Dominici (2019)
    <https://jmlr.org/papers/v20/18-760.html>. The separation of tuning from
    final holdout evaluation is motivated by Cawley and Talbot (2010)
    <https://jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html>.
